steps.message:
LegacyConfig stays a public static helper, so I kept `public static class LegacyConfig` and the `Read(string path)` signature and put a scoped filesystem seam behind it. The obstacle is that `Read` calls `File.Exists` and `File.ReadAllText` directly, which forces a deterministic test to touch real disk. Since callers cannot change and the API cannot take a parameter, I introduced a minimal ambient file-store abstraction that exposes only the two members `Read` uses.

```csharp
public interface IFileStore
{
    bool Exists(string path);
    string ReadAllText(string path);
}

file sealed class SystemFileStore : IFileStore
{
    public bool Exists(string path) => File.Exists(path);
    public string ReadAllText(string path) => File.ReadAllText(path);
}

public static class LegacyConfig
{
    private static readonly IFileStore s_default = new SystemFileStore();
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<IFileStore?> s_store = new();

    private static IFileStore Store => s_store.Value ?? s_default;

    public static string? Read(string path) =>
        Store.Exists(path) ? Store.ReadAllText(path) : null;

    public static IDisposable OverrideStore(IFileStore store)
    {
        var previous = s_store.Value;   // remember the store active in THIS scope
        s_store.Value = store;
        return new Restore(() => s_store.Value = previous);
    }

    private sealed class Restore(Action onDispose) : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => onDispose();
    }
}
```

The default field is a real `System.IO`-backed store, so production still reads and writes actual files and preserves the original null-when-missing behavior. The override lives in an `AsyncLocal<IFileStore?>` so it flows across `await` and stays isolated between concurrent async flows; `OverrideStore` captures the previous store and restores it on disposal rather than clearing the slot, which is what makes nested scopes correct.

Tests use only an in-memory dictionary-backed `IFileStore` and never call `File.ReadAllText` or write temp files:

- A nested test opens an outer override whose in-memory store maps a path to one payload, opens an inner override with a second in-memory store mapping the same path to a different payload, asserts `Read` returns the inner payload, then disposes the inner scope and asserts `Read` returns the outer payload again, proving inner disposal restores the outer store instead of falling back to disk.
- A missing-key test asserts `Read` returns null when the in-memory store reports the path absent, matching the production contract.
- A parallel test runs multiple `Task.Run` flows, each with its own in-memory store, and awaits `Task.WhenAll(...)`; each flow reads only its own content, proving the async flows stay isolated.

Built the production project first, then ran `dotnet test fixtures/static-config-reader/tests/StaticConfigReader.Tests.csproj`; all tests pass with no real filesystem access.
